### 🚀 Feature Proposal

Since approval of [ECMAScript Explicit Resource Management](https://github.com/tc39/proposal-explicit-resource-management) and its already [adoption by Typescript 5.2](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-5-2.html#using-declarations-and-explicit-resource-management) and an already usage of `Symbol.asyncDispose` in https://github.com/fastify/fastify/pull/5082. We could introduce another support point of this proposal in `decorate()` where upon server shutdown, any available `Symbol.asyncDispose` or `Symbol.dispose` would be automatically called.

### Motivation

This could improve the lifetime and resource management of resources that should be attached to an http server.

### Example

```ts
class MyDatabase {
  // Closes the database connection
  [Symbol.asyncDispose]() {}
}

app.decorate('database', new MyDatabase());

// when app closes, myDatabase will close as well.
```

or 

```ts
class MyRedisEventEmitter {
  // Ensures every event was sent upstream
  [Symbol.dispose]() {}
}

app.decorate('emitter', new MyRedisEventEmitter());

// when app closes, myRedisEventEmitter  will close as well.
```

or anything else that needs to be closed upon server shutdown but previously had to be done manually (and possibly forgotten).

Starting from this PR https://github.com/fastify/fastify/pull/4264

Right now, if the user wants to serialize a response has too many options based on the needs.

### Case 1: serialize a non JSON payload

The dev must provide a [reply serializer](https://www.fastify.io/docs/latest/Reference/Reply/#serializerfunc) or an [encapsulated reply serializer](https://www.fastify.io/docs/latest/Reference/Server/#setreplyserializer).

Downsides:

- when the reply serializer is used, you must set it every time
- when the encapsulated reply serializer is set, other handles in the same context cannot return other payloads (unless setting the reply serializer every time)
- whatever the `content-type` is, the reply serializer will be executed
- there is no clear source of truth for serialize a response

```
function handler(request, reply){
  reply
    .header('Content-Type', 'application/x-protobuf')
    .serializer(protoBuf.serialize)
}

fastify.setReplySerializer(function (payload, statusCode){
  // serialize the payload with a sync function
  return `my serialized ${statusCode} content: ${payload}`
})
```

### Case 2: serialize a JSON payload

A developer that needs to reply with a JSON payload has nothing to do.
But, if the developer needs to apply a different schema based on the `content-type` must set the `schema` with a special structure:

This setting builds a new serialization function for every `content-type`

```js
const schema = {
  response: {
    200: {
      content: {
        'application/json': {
          schema: {}
        },
        'application/vnd.v1+json': {
          schema: {}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Proposal


- Add the `content-type` to the reply serialize option, so Fastify will decide which one to run based on the `reply.type`
- Remove the `reply.serialize()` method. It will impact:
  - [ ] fastify-compress
- The json schema `content` config will add a new reply serializer by calling `setReplySerializer` but limited to the route itself. If the reply serializer is already defined, the JSON schema will win _(or should we throw an error?)_

Example:

```js
fastify.setReplySerializer('application/x-protobuf', function (payload, statusCode){
  return protoBuf.serialize(payload)
})

fastify.setReplySerializer('application/vnd.v1+json', function (payload, statusCode){
  return JSON.stringify(payload)
})

const schema = {
  response: {
    200: {
      content: {
        'application/json': {
          schema: {}
        },
        'application/vnd.v1+json': {
          schema: {}
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fastify.get('/', {
  schema,
  replySerializers: {
    'application/vnd.v1+json': function (payload, statusCode){
      return JSON.stringify(payload)
    }
  }
}, handler)

function handler(request, reply){
  reply.header('Content-Type', 'application/x-protobuf')
       .send({ foo: "bar" })
}
```

