### Description

When you try to use `reply.send()` in the `onRequest`-Hook, fastify also tries to finish the normal lifecycle for a request at the same time.

Internally `runPreParsing` is getting called after the onRequest-Hook to proceed with the normal lifecycle of an incoming request. And because it doesn't know about the request already being handled differently, it also continues on it .. resulting in weird errors and or unexpected responses.

It's very easy to reproduce it. Especially if you make use of other hooks that are being also called when you make use of reply.send() that need some time to process.

### Steps to Reproduce

can be reproduced with this code:

```js
import { fastify } from "fastify";

const f = fastify({ logger: { transport: { target: "pino-pretty", options: { singleLine: true } } } });

f.setNotFoundHandler(async (request, reply) => { reply.code(404).send("nothing here"); });

// simply answer any request: status 200 & and a random object (date in this case)
f.addHook("onRequest", async (request, reply) => { reply.code(200).send(new Date()); });

// also add another hook that actually should not be executed
f.addHook("preParsing", async (request, reply) => { request.log.info("preParsing: should not be called here - but happens"); });

// do some serializing .. it may takes some time
f.addHook("preSerialization", async (request, reply, payload) => {
    return await new Promise(async (resolve) => {
        setTimeout(() => {
            const serialized = payload.getFullYear();
            request.log.info("finished serialization here .. " + serialized);
            resolve(serialized);
        }, 1000);
    });
});

f.listen({ host: "0.0.0.0", port: "3500" });
```

**output:** 
```
